<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Harar Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1647 CE – 1887 CE · capital Harar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A walled Muslim city-state in eastern Ethiopia, a centre of Islamic learning and trade, which kept its independence until Menelik II took it in 1887.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal</a> leadership moved to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-aussa">Imamate of Aussa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> was left to local governors. In 1647 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-daud">Ali ibn Daud</a> founded an independent dynasty, ruling the city and its farmlands amid the Oromo-settled countryside. The city walls, built in the 16th century by Emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-ibn-mujahid">Nur ibn Mujahid</a>, enclosed the old town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugol">Jugol</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> was a city of mosques, shrines and scholars, its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Harari</a> language written in Arabic script. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> minted coins and taxed the caravans carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, khat, slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a>. They made alliances with surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> groups. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton">Richard Burton</a>, the first European known to enter, visited in 1855.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> from 1875 to 1885, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Abdullahi ibn Ali briefly restored independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chelenqo">Battle of Chelenqo</a> in 1887 and annexed Harar to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>. The old walled town is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/harar-emirate.jpg" alt="Harar Emirate" width="320" height="250"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Augustus Mitchell Jr. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horn_of_Africa_and_Emirate_Harar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1647 CE – 1887 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Harar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali ibn Daud, Abdullahi ibn Ali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Harari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Harar, Sultanate of Harar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1767&amp;focus=harar-emirate" aria-label="Harar Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5843 1830 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Harar Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6163 2086L6135 2044M6163 2086L5811 1337M6163 2086L6029 1964"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6135 2044h0M5811 1337h0M6029 1964h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6163 2086h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6163 2086h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Harar. <a href="/atlas?year=1767&amp;focus=harar-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
